WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.270
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here, and today we’re
talking about one of the more thoughtful Marvel

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:15.059
properties out there: Daredevil. And no, NOT the Ben
Affleck one. Also, spoiler alert for all of you that

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:19.440
haven’t binged both seasons yet. Matt Murdock,
Blind Lawyer, and human lie detector… is

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.789
a vigilante: the Devil of Hell’s Kitchen.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:25.990
The very concept of justice is based in the
idea that law ought to be fair. This is why

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:31.179
justice is personified as a blind woman holding
scales and a sword: to weigh guilt and to

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.890
execute said justice. Its no coincidence that
Matt Murdock is a blind lawyer that beats

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.610
people up in his Pajamas.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.030
The other element the show places significant
emphasis on is the fact that Matt is a Catholic.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.430
The choice to name a catholic superhero Matthew
after the Apostle and Evangelist is quite

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.579
apt. As a tax collector Matthew would

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:52.980
have a similar reputation to lawyers today.
The Book of Matthew does quite a bit of commentary

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.219
on greed and social justice. Matthew 25: 31-
46 sounds a whole lot like a justification

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:03.320
for Pro Bono Legal work and Matthew 23:16
deals explicitly with corruption and blindness.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.160
In fact, the promotional material for the
second season was heavily influenced by religious

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.070
art including Caravvagio Michaelangelo’s
The Last Judgement, and Rubens’s Saint Sebastian,

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:26.180
which echoes Claire’s fears about Matt. Whether
it be divine law or man-made law, the central

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.490
theme of Daredevil is Justice. So lets get
to it, Welcome to this Wisecrack edition on

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:38.470
The Philosophy of Daredevil. Daredevil addresses
a serious problem with justice: legal liability,

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.890
whether or not a guilty party ought to be
held responsible for their actions.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.340
Sure it seems simple enough, somebody goes
on a killing spree… they deserve to be punished,

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.250
right? But that narrative is complicated by
one of the central questions in Daredevil:

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.140
Are people responsible for their life situations?
In season two, Nelson and Murdock attempt

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.610
to use PTSD as a defense for Frank Castle,
saying that Frank doesn’t know the difference

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:07.870
between right and wrong, and thus, he’s
not responsible for his actions.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:14.159
When little Frankie Castle balks at this,
they reword their defense, to “sympathetic

00:02:14.159 --> 00:02:19.540
storming”, a diagnosis where the sympathetic
system is on a heightened state of alert due

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:26.480
to extreme trauma and emotional disturbance.
Again, the idea is: Frank Castle is not responsible

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.250
for his actions. The show asks: is a person
still to blame for their actions if they aren’t

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.239
free to chose the person they have become?

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:38.819
Wilson Fisk is conditioned into a world of
violence by his abusive father until he, well,

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:43.260
snaps. Electra is presented as an emotionless
killing machine that can do nothing but murder

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:50.069
because of her nature as the Black Sky. Elektra
is literally predisposed to be evil, and a

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:53.549
significant portion of the second season revolves
around the question: can she exercise her

00:02:53.549 --> 00:02:55.959
free will to deny her destiny?

00:02:55.959 --> 00:03:02.530
Frank Castle has experienced the complete
horror of war, only to return home and see

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:07.450
his entire family violently gunned down. Can
one really blame him for being vengeful?

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:11.830
And then there's Karen. We've already seen her murder one person. And the show constantly hints

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.130
at her mysterious dark past.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:25.140
Karen has to believe that someone like Frank Castle isn't all bad. That he isn't defined by his deeds.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.640
Because if he is, she is, and always will be a murderer.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:41.459
Philosophically speaking, the idea that all decisions are
determined by previous causes is called determinism.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:45.150
Determinists don’t really believe in free
will. All actions are just the consequence

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:50.129
of previous actions. Each choice a person
makes is impacted by previous life events.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:54.239
In a deterministic universe, concepts like
responsibility and punishment don’t really

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:57.909
make sense because if we’re just a product
of our past, then we don’t really CHOOSE

00:03:57.909 --> 00:04:01.999
to do the things we do. And how can we be
punished for something we didn’t choose?

00:04:01.999 --> 00:04:06.109
Matt represents a different view of criminal
justice: Libertarianism: which believes in

00:04:06.109 --> 00:04:11.349
the ability to choose other than your determined
nature. Libertarians are of the hopeful sorts.

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:16.150
I mean… Matt literally wears rose-colored
glasses. Matt presents an option whereby people

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:22.150
can elevate themselves beyond their determined
condition or environment. It’s what Matt

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:28.170
is going on an on about on the roof with the
Punisher, about the redemptive spark inside of

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:32.530
a person, where someone isn’t all bad,
that they can choose of their own free will

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:33.780
to be different.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.290
While Matt is supposed to be the example to
prove determinism wrong: Matt has the devil

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:46.600
inside him, whatever that means. He wants to kill
people like Fisk, but he chooses not to. Does

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:55.660
he act of his own free will or is Matt merely
un-free in his Catholicism? Is his choice

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.230
not to kill the Kingpin a decision that he
makes freely or does he merely make this decision

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:04.750
based on the events that led to him accepting
Catholicism and all of its precepts?

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:17.040
Vigilantism causes controversy because the
vigilante, being outside the legal system,

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.840
doesn’t have authority to distribute punishment.
There’s no due process or impartiality.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.560
Vigilantism is common in comic books but in
most cases the hero isn’t in a position

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.600
to be harmed by being outed as a vigilante.
Electra is silly rich. Bruce Wayne and Tony

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.260
Stark are billionaire playboys. Superman can
get shot in the eyeball and be fine. Matt

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:40.740
Murdock on the otherhand, is a blind defense lawyer who gets paid in bananas and casseroles.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:47.380
There is actual risk for Matt if people find out who he is.
It’s part of why the Daredevil is the man without fear.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:53.660
Matt’s motivation for risking so much is
echoed by a Thurgood Marshall speech from 1992 about dissenting against fear.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.350
While justice Marshall may not be calling
for vigilante justice he is calling for an

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:10.000
end to fear and apathy—to not turn a “blind
eye” to injustice. Though vigilante violence

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.970
is still extra-legal, the question: what makes
a person good is a central idea of the show—and

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:27.650
while it’s a little heavy handed—they
make it clear that Matt Murdock is just a

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:33.770
bad day away from becoming Wilson Fisk or
Frank castle.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.840
Most superhero narratives play up the cliché
of good and evil. There’s a pure heroic

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.120
protagonist and a corrupt and vile antagonist.
In Daredevil, however, this elementary distinction

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:50.750
is blurred by Kingpin, Stick, Electra, and
the Punisher, who all occupy moral gray areas.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:59.100
They don’t set out to do evil, they validate
their decisions by appeals to a greater good.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:04.180
For the Kingpin it is the glory of his city. For the Punisher, he is merely doling out justice, for Stick

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:08.680
and Electra there is a great war between the
Chaste and the Hand. That they have to deal with.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:14.030
What separates Matt from Karen, The punisher
and the Kingpin is faith— Matt is a Catholic,

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:23.000
whereas all the others profess they aren’t
religious. Orphaned after the death of his

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.260
father, he is raised by nuns, attends church
regularly and makes an effort to be a good

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:33.550
man. Which is funny because Matt chooses to
dress up like the devil and kick the shit

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:38.320
out of people. And so for Karen, The Punisher,
and Kingpin there is nothing besides the corrupt

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.150
law to keep them from killing. No divine law
that makes taking life unjust.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:47.750
Each season Matt tackles different adversaries,
but the actual enemy doesn’t really matter.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.850
What matters is Matt’s struggle with his
inner devil—with his desire to bring Justice

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:57.120
to his city by any means necessary… except
killing… Matt is a good catholic boy who

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.630
likes hurting people but has a real problem
killing people. Something about “Thou shall

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:02.290
not kill”?

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.540
Except that he doesn’t choose not to kill—he
kills Nobu three times but conveniently forgets

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:15.200
about it every time he does so. He lights
him on fire, he throws him off a three-story

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:19.180
balcony, then he chucks him off of a roof,
and at no time does he express any guilt for

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.080
doing so… so maybe he does have the devil
in him…

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.860
A great example of the dichotomy of Matt's relationship to the law happens early in season 1.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:34.200
When he exercises his ability as a defense attorney to defend a murderer he knows is guilty, then turns around

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.320
and doles out some street justice as soon as he leaves the courthouse.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.160
Or how he fights the Punisher and then defends him in court in season 2.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.319
There is 
a constant refrain from characters in the
show and that is: Daredevil’s tactics as

00:08:45.319 --> 00:08:55.089
a vigilante are half measures. Fisk argues
that what’s dangerous about Matt is not

00:08:55.089 --> 00:08:59.410
that he wears a cape or mask, Daredevil is
dangerous because he believes one man can

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:09.370
make 
a difference. For Fisk, it’s only through
sweeping structural changes that come about

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:14.680
by through influence; power, money and eating
the same omelet everyday that real change can take

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.269
shape.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:24.019
Fisks’s argument is echoed by both The Punisher
and Stick: that Daredevil’s vigilantism

00:09:24.019 --> 00:09:28.860
is insufficient. Matt plods through life like
his father, a boxer who slides through life

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:34.180
taking on each opponent one by one, fight
by fight. For Stick and Punisher, Daredevil’s

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:44.620
insistence to not kill limits his ability
to effectuate change. Daredevil’s perps

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:52.370
end up back on the street to continue to rape,
kill, and murder. In many ways the yellow

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:56.709
seen everywhere can be read as more than just
an homage to the primary colors of the comic

00:09:56.709 --> 00:10:00.939
books: it speaks to what Daredevil’s frenemies
imply… that The Devil of Hell’s kitchen

00:10:00.939 --> 00:10:02.999
is really a yellowbellied coward.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:12.420
In a criminal justice sense, Daredevil justifies
his actions through a utilitarian approach,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:13.740
His idea of justice relies

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:18.600
on rehabilitating, incapacitating and deterring
the criminal. Punishment is justified by a

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.430
forward-looking prediction to stop future
harm for the sake of all of society. An action

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:28.379
is just only if it advances one of these goals.
Like imprisoning the Kingpin and the Punisher.

00:10:28.379 --> 00:10:33.160
Sure there isn’t due process, a jury, or
a judge but this is a question of philosophical

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:37.610
justification. Daredevil does what’s needed
in a predictive sense—he protects the neighborhood

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:42.480
by beating the sh*t out of people. Whereas
the Punisher represents a  more retributive concept

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.170
of justice which closely aligns with a deontological
or Kantian approach to justice.

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:52.300
For Immanuel Kant, it’s not a question of
protecting society based on the consequences

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:55.240
of punishment. Retributive justice is concerned
with deserts, no not ice cream, just deserts:

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.990
getting what a person deserves. Punishment
isn’t used to help society so much as it

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:02.970
is to assure that a person gets what they
are due. In fact, for the retributivist it

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:09.749
would be UNJUST or immoral to do otherwise.
It is similar to the concept of an ‘eye

00:11:09.749 --> 00:11:14.100
for an eye’ or Lex Talionis: A system of
justice “which requires the infliction upon

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:16.930
a wrongdoer of the same injury he has caused
another.

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:22.269
So, yeah, the Punisher… Punishes people.
Whether it’s the Kitchen Irish, the Dogs

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:28.319
of hell or members of his ex-military outfit,
Punisher acts to right the wrong done to his

00:11:28.319 --> 00:11:32.629
family. There is no legal recourse—he ‘just
does’ … he insures that people get what

00:11:32.629 --> 00:11:34.730
they deserve.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:39.100
Daredevil presents the story of a conflicted
hero, torn between his duty to the church,

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:43.220
the legal profession, and his desire to stand
up for those that are unable to stand up for

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:47.790
themselves. Sure Daredevil may be a Batman
rip off but it did generate the source material

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:51.509
to create the teenage mutant ninja turtles.
While all of this is great, what makes Daredevil

00:11:51.509 --> 00:11:56.559
a phenomenal series is that at its heart the
show is about kicking ass. In a hallway, in

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:01.460
alleys, stairwells, living rooms, and cellblocks...
it just kicks ass.

